There is a doorknob that still opens
into a wound, where my father, unimpressed
by a child’s adoration of fire, let me
press my one-year-old palm into the yellow face
of a lantern. My hero who was startled
when, at 17, I fled from home, as if
his face were the face of the yellow lantern.
Who munched fish in the warm river
of his mouth and fed them to me.
Who worked long hours that curdled into days,
until the river in his veins throbbed.
He taught me how to swallow my river,
and bend my ribcage into a canoe of bones.
How could I not call him on Father’s Day
after all these years, my phone pressed to my ear
as if eavesdropping on his heartbeat.
Two unbroken men, breaking in static.
Because sometimes not breaking like an egg
is only a form of rottenness.
